Two bugs:
1. HTMLParser → ["htm", "lparser"] — the acronym boundary detection is wrong. When we see 'P' after "HTML", `prev_is_upper is true, next_is_lower ('a') is true, current.len() > 1` ("HTML" has len 4) — so it pops the last char 'L', pushes "HTM", starts new word with "LP"... The issue is that `chars[i-1]` is 'L' which is uppercase (correct), but we're checking the wrong index. The prev_is_upper check should look at whether the current accumulator ends with uppercase, not chars[i-1].

Actually wait, the issue is simpler: when we hit 'P', current = "HTML". We pop 'L', push "HTM", then set current = "L" then push 'P' → current = "LP". Then 'a', 'r', 's', 'e', 'r' get appended → "LParser". That's wrong — it should be "html" and "parser". The pop logic is off by one: when we see uppercase 'P' after all-caps "HTML" with lowercase 'a' following, the split should be "HTML" → keep as word, then start new word with 'P'. Wait no, the correct split is HTML|Parser, so when we see 'P' at position 4, we need to realize the boundary is between 'L' and 'P'.

Actually, looking again: `current = "HTML", we see 'P' (uppercase). prev_is_upper` = true (chars[3] = 'L' is uppercase). next_is_lower = true (chars[5] = 'a'). current.len() > 1 = true. So we pop 'L' from current → current becomes "HTM", push "HTM" as word. Then current = "L", push 'P' → current = "LP". Then lowercase chars follow → "LParser".

The bug: we shouldn't pop the last char. The boundary is between the current run and the new word starting at the current position. For "HTMLParser", when we hit 'P': the previous uppercase run is "HTML" but the 'L' actually belongs with 'P' since "LP..." starts "LParser" — no wait, that's wrong too. The correct split is "HTML" + "Parser". So when we hit 'P' (uppercase, with 'a' lowercase after), we should push the entire current "HTML" and start fresh with "P".

The problem is the algorithm assumes the last char of the uppercase run belongs to the next word (because in "getURLValue", the 'V' starts "Value"). But "HTMLParser" — the 'L' is the last char of the acronym "HTML", and 'P' starts "Parser". So the split should happen *before* 'P', keeping all of "HTML".

The fix: when we have consecutive uppercase followed by an uppercase + lowercase, the boundary is right before the current char, not one char back. Let me reconsider. The standard convention:

• HTMLParser → HTML, Parser (split before P, because P starts a new word Pa...)  
• getURLValue → get, URL, Value (split before V, keeping URL together)

The rule: when at position i with uppercase char, and i-1 was uppercase, and i+1 is lowercase — the boundary is before position i. So we push the full `current` and start a new word with the current char.
